      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\(s_2\\), we first need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\(s_2\\) after it is translated from segment \\(s_1\\).\n\nThe endpoint of segment \\(s_1\\) is \\((4, 1)\\). When this endpoint is translated 2 units to the right and 3 units up, the new coordinates are:\n\\[\n(4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n\\]\nSo, the first endpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) is \\((6, 4)\\).\n\nThe other endpoint of segment \\(s_1\\) is \\((-8, 5)\\). When this endpoint is translated 2 units to the right and 3 units up, the new coordinates are:\n\\[\n(-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n\\]\nSo, the second endpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) is \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nThe midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\(s_2\\) into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
