      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\). The endpoints of \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\). To find the midpoint of \\( s_2 \\), we will use the midpoint formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nHere, \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) are the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\). For segment \\( s_1 \\), the coordinates are \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the midpoint of the endpoints of \\( s_1 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{4 + (-8)}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{4 - 8}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2} \\right) = \\left( -2, 3 \\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_1 \\) is \\((-2, 3)\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\). Segment \\( s_2 \\) is obtained by translating segment \\( s_1 \\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up. This means we add 2 to the x-coordinate and 3 to the y-coordinate of each endpoint of \\( s_1 \\):\n\nThe new coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\) are:\n\n\\[\n(4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n\\]\n\\[\n(-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the midpoint of these new endpoints:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 - 6}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = \\left( 0, 6 \\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\((0, 6)\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 6)}\n\\]",
