      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to find the midpoint of segment \\( s_1 \\) and then apply the translation to this midpoint to find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\).\n\nThe formula for the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nFor segment \\( s_1 \\) with endpoints \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\), the midpoint is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{4 + (-8)}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2} \\right) = (-2, 3)\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the translation to this midpoint. Translating a point \\((x, y)\\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up results in the point \\((x + 2, y + 3)\\). Therefore, translating the midpoint \\((-2, 3)\\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up gives:\n\\[\n(-2 + 2, 3 + 3) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
